  "technology_name": "Copper-Nickel Nanocomposite: Bimetallic Water Purification Material",
  "institution": "KSCSTE- Centre for Water Resources Development and Management (CWRDM)",
  "primary_sector": "Water, Environment & Waste Management",
  "technology_type": "Material/Formulation, Device/Equipment",
  "problem_solved": "Arsenic, heavy metal, and bacterial contamination in water requiring stable, scalable, and reusable purification methods.",
  "description": "A water purification technology utilizing bimetallic copper-nickel nanoparticles (CuNi BNPs) embedded within porous styrene-divinylbenzene (St-DVB) polymer microbeads. Copper provides low toxicity and antimicrobial properties, while nickel offers strong chemical and magnetic characteristics. Combining them enhances heavy metal and bacterial removal, particularly for arsenic adsorption. Immobilizing the nanoparticles on reusable polymer microbeads prevents aggregation and increases the active surface area. The resulting nanocomposite operates efficiently in continuous adsorption column systems, providing a stable, low-cost, and scalable method for water treatment across a wide pH range.",
  "applications": "• Heavy metal and arsenic removal in municipal water treatment facilities\n\n• Continuous adsorption column systems for industrial effluent purification\n\n• Point-of-use domestic water filtration devices for rural and urban settings\n\n• Commercial production of reusable, porous polymer adsorbent microbeads",
